Double circulation
Mammals run TWO circuits. PULMONARY (lungs): right atrium → right ventricle → PULMONARY ARTERY (carries DEOXYGENATED blood — the great exception) → lungs (CO2 out, O2 in) → PULMONARY VEIN (OXYGENATED — the other exception) → left atrium. SYSTEMIC (body): left atrium → LEFT VENTRICLE (thickest wall — pumps body-wide at high pressure) → AORTA → body tissues (O2/glucose out, CO2/waste in) → VENA CAVA → right atrium. VALVES enforce one-way flow: bicuspid/mitral (left), tricuspid (right), and semilunar valves at artery exits (their snap shut makes the heartbeat's 'lub-dub'). Coronary arteries feed the heart muscle itself — their blockage causes heart attacks. VESSELS: arteries (thick muscle/elastic, small lumen, high pressure, pulse); veins (thin walls, wide lumen, VALVES, low pressure); capillaries (ONE cell thick, narrow — exchange only, white cells squeeze through).

Blood and pressure
RED CELLS: biconcave discs, NO nucleus, packed with HAEMOGLOBIN — maximum oxygen loading; made in bone marrow, living 120 days (malaria and sickle cell destroy them). WHITE CELLS: larger, with nuclei — PHAGOCYTES engulf germs, LYMPHOCYTES make ANTIBODIES (vaccination trains them). PLATELETS: cell fragments triggering CLOTTING (fibrin mesh seals wounds; haemophilia breaks this). PLASMA: pale fluid carrying dissolved glucose, amino acids, hormones, urea, antibodies and heat. BLOOD PRESSURE: force on artery walls (~120/80 mmHg normal). HYPERTENSION (persistent high pressure — salt, obesity, stress, genetics) silently damages heart, brain (stroke), kidneys and eyes: the 'silent killer' — preventable by less salt, healthy weight, exercise and prescribed drugs taken faithfully.$wdthseed$, NULL, 40, 3, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Trace blood through the heart naming chambers, vessels and valves","Compare arteries, veins and capillaries structurally","State the functions of red cells, white cells, platelets and plasma"],"introduction":"Your heart beats 100,000 times a day, pushing 7,000 litres through 100,000 km of vessels. It is two pumps in one — and understanding it explains blood pressure, heart attacks and why exercise matters.","key_points":["Full heart trace without skipping","Pulmonary exceptions","Blood component functions"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 34 (circulatory system)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod08$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod08:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
