Structure and strategies
Flower parts: SEPALS (green, protect the bud) → PETALS (often bright/scented, attract insects) → STAMENS (male: anther makes POLLEN, filament holds it) → CARPEL(s) (female: stigma catches pollen, style connects, ovary holds OVULES with egg cells). INSECT-pollinated flowers: large bright petals, scent, nectar guides, sticky/spiky pollen, sticky stigma. WIND-pollinated (maize, grasses): tiny dull petals, NO scent/nectar, HUGE pollen quantities, light smooth grains, large FEATHERY stigmas dangling to catch drifting pollen, anthers dangling outside. POLLINATION = pollen landing on stigma (self: same flower/plant — fast, no variation; cross: different plant — variation, needs couriers). FERTILISATION follows: pollen germinates, a tube grows down the style, male nuclei enter the ovule — one fuses with the EGG (zygote → embryo), one fuses with endosperm nuclei (food store) — flowering plants' unique DOUBLE fertilisation.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 5, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Label sepals, petals, stamens, carpels and their sub-parts","Contrast insect- and wind-pollinated flowers with four features each","Describe the pollen tube journey to the ovule"],"introduction":"A flower is advertising, engineering and nursery in one: colours and scents bribe couriers, structures load and receive pollen, and ovaries become the fruits we eat.","key_points":["Four flower whorls + sub-parts","Insect vs wind feature tables","Pollination ≠ fertilisation"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 32, Plant Reproduction"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod09$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod09:top03$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_