Dispersal and the seed
After fertilisation, the OVARY wall becomes the FRUIT and OVULES become SEEDS (embryo + food store + protective TESTA). Dispersal methods: WIND — light, winged/plumed seeds (Tridax daisy fluff, mahogany wings); ANIMALS — tasty fruits eaten and dropped (mango, guava) or hooked burrs hitching fur/clothes (grasses); WATER — floating fibrous fruits (coconut husks ride oceans); EXPLOSIVE — pods flinging seeds (oil bean/pentaclethra, peas). Seeds contain the EMBRYO (plumule → shoot, radicle → root) plus food (endosperm or fat cotyledons — beans' fleshy halves). GERMINATION needs three conditions: WATER (activates enzymes, softens testa), OXYGEN (aerobic respiration for growth energy), and WARMTH (enzyme optimum) — light is NOT required (most seeds germinate underground; its absence even helps some).$wdthseed$, NULL, 30, 6, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Explain four seed-dispersal mechanisms with examples","Label a seed (testa, embryo, food store)","State germination requirements and how to test each"],"introduction":"A mango seed cannot walk to a new clearing — so the fruit bribes you to carry it. Dispersal is the plant kingdom's travel industry.","key_points":["Four dispersal types + examples","Three germination conditions","One-variable fair tests"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 32 (seed development, germination)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod09$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod09:top03$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, 