Iodine stains nuclei/starch; methylene blue stains animal nuclei.
Draw: large, single pencil lines, no shading, ruled touching labels, titled with magnification.
Honesty rule: draw the slide you see, not the textbook you remember.$wdthseed$, NULL, NULL, 4, TRUE, TRUE, (select id from public.lessons where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod10:top01:les02$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set block_type = excluded.block_type, title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, url = excluded.url, duration_seconds = excluded.duration_seconds, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id;

Model guts and potato cylinders
VISKING TUBING: a partially permeable membrane. Fill a tube with starch + glucose solution, seal it, place in distilled water: after 20 minutes, test the OUTER water — Benedict's goes positive (small glucose diffused OUT) while iodine stays brown (large starch stayed IN) — proving selective permeability by size. Test the INNER solution with iodine (still blue-black — starch trapped). YAM/POTATO CYLINDERS: identical cores in a RANGE of salt/sugar concentrations (0, 5, 10, 20%) for fixed time; measure MASS or LENGTH change → plot % change vs concentration → the curve CROSSES ZERO at the tissue's own concentration (isotonic point — read it off the graph). Fair-test essentials: identical cores (same borer, blot before weighing), same volumes/temperatures/times, repeats + means. Controls: distilled water (maximum gain) anchors the scale.

Tables and graphs that score
TABLES: ruled, with independent variable (concentration) in the LEFT column, dependent (mass change) next, units in HEADERS not cells ('Mass change / g'), repeats then a MEAN column, and a descriptive TITLE above. GRAPHS: independent variable on X, dependent on Y; labelled axes WITH units; even scales using 3/4+ of the paper; precise small plots (x or +); best-fit LINE (trends) or smooth CURVE (rates) — never dot-to-dot for continuous data; TITLE below. Calculate % change as (change ÷ original) × 100 — percentages allow fair comparison between different-sized cores. Read INTERCEPTS (zero-crossing = isotonic point) and GRADIENTS (steeper = faster osmosis) — graphs are arguments, not decorations.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 3, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Run a Visking-tubing model gut and interpret it","Measure osmosis by mass, length or bending","Present results in tables and graphs with full headings"],"introduction":"A sausage skin of Visking tubing filled with starch models your entire small intestine. Dialysis machines are just this idea, scaled up to save lives.","key_points":["Visking predictions both sides","Isotonic interpolation","Table/graph marking rules"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 5 (osmosis investigations)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod10$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod10:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, 